    "knowledge_point": "Computing Second Order Derivatives",
    "problem": "Find ${g}^{″}(4)$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7.$",
    "worked_solution": "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{5/2}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{2}){x}^{3/2}-2+0 \\\\  & =10{x}^{3/2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & ={(10{x}^{3/2}-2)}^{′} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{1/2}-0 \\\\  & =15{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(4) & =15\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =15(2) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
